1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
let's talk about authoring an MCP server. MCP gives you three primitives to expose,

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
tools, resources, and prompts. They look similar, but each answers to a different controller,

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
and that ownership split is the distinction developers sometimes get wrong. This lesson

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
walks through all three and explains how they work. Let's name the three primitives an MCP

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:29,000
server exposes. First, tools. These are executable functions the model can call,

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:35,000
like an API request or a database query. They are the actions your server offers. Second,

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:42,000
resources. These are read-only data sources, file contents, records, an API response,

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:50,000
each one identified by a URI. They supply context, they never do anything. Third, prompts. These are

9
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,000
reusable templates, and the user picks them, usually as a slash command or a menu item in

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:02,000
the host. Tools act, resources inform, prompts get selected. Hold those three apart because the

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:07,000
next thing we do is pin down who controls each one, and that is where it gets interesting.

12
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Here's the idea that matters more than any field name. Each primitive answers to a different

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
controller. Tools are model-controlled, which means Cloud itself decides when to call one

14
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
based on the request in front of it. Resources are application-controlled, so the host application

15
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,000
decides which ones to pull in as context. The model doesn't reach out and grab a resource.

16
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Prompts are user-controlled, meaning a person deliberately selects the template.

17
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:43,000
The model never triggers a prompt on its own. The model controls tools, the application controls

18
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:49,000
resources, the user controls prompts. Cross-attributing them is the single most common

19
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,000
MCP mistake, so match each primitive to its controller first and everything downstream

20
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:01,000
stays clear. Let's lock the ownership in with the three controllers side by side. First,

21
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:07,000
tools are controlled by the model. Cloud discovers what tools exist and invokes them on its own,

22
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:13,000
reading the request and the tool's description to decide. Second, resources are controlled by

23
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:20,000
the application. The host decides which resources to inject as context on its own terms, not because

24
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,000
the model asked. Third, prompts are controlled by the user. A person picks a prompt template

25
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:34,000
deliberately, and the model never fires one by itself. Model, application, user. Three primitives,

26
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,000
three controllers. That mapping is the thing to carry out of this lesson,

27
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,000
because almost every MCP question turns on it. Let's go deeper on tools, because they're the

28
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,000
primitive that acts. A tool is a callable action. Behind it sits an API call, a database query,

29
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:56,000
or a file operation, something that does work. The model is in charge here. It discovers the

30
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:02,000
tools your server advertises, then invokes them based on the conversation and the user's request.

31
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Exactly the way tool calling works in the Cloud API. In fact, an MCP tool mirrors the tool use

32
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:14,000
contract you already know. The model emits a call, your server runs it, and the result comes back.

33
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,000
So when you register a tool, you're handing Cloud an action it can choose to take on its own.

34
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Now, resources. And this is where people slip. A resource is read-only context.

35
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Think file contents, a database record, or an API response, each one identified by a URI,

36
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:40,000
so the client can address it. Here's the key part. Resources are application controlled.

37
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:45,000
The host application decides when to read a resource and inject it into the context.

38
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:51,000
The model doesn't go fetch a resource on its own, the way it calls a tool. And because a resource

39
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:57,000
only supplies data, it never carries out an action. If you catch yourself saying a resource

40
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:04,000
runs something or changes state, stop. That's a tool's job. Resources inform, they don't act.

41
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:11,000
That brings us to prompts, the primitive that belongs to the user. A prompt is a reusable

42
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:17,000
template, a pre-built message or workflow the server offers. The user is the one who reaches

43
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:23,000
for it. Hosts usually surface prompts as slash commands or menu entries, so a person can pick

44
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:29,000
one deliberately. The model doesn't decide to run a prompt, and that's the whole point. A prompt is

45
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:36,000
an invitation the user accepts, not an action the model takes. So keep the line clear. Tools are the

46
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:43,000
models to call, resources are the applications to inject, and prompts are the users to select.

47
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:49,000
Same server, three very different kinds of control. Let's clear up a naming trap that

48
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:56,000
bites developers who know the Cloud API well. In MCP, when you define a tool, its parameters live

49
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:03,000
under input schema, spelled in camel case. You'll also see is error and the method tools slash call

50
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:10,000
written the same way, because MCP rides on JSON RPC. Now compare that to the Cloud Messages API,

51
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:16,000
where the same idea is input schema. In snake case, right next to is error and tool use.

52
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Same concepts, different layer, different spelling. The MCP wire format and the Messages API are two

53
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,000
distinct things, and conflating their field names is an easy way to trip yourself up.

54
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:35,000
When you're authoring a server, match the MCP spelling. When you're calling the API,

55
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:43,000
match the APIs. One more thing worth stepping back for. MCP is an open model agnostic standard.

56
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:49,000
It isn't a cloud only feature, and it isn't just another single API you call. That matters because

57
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:55,000
of reuse. One MCP server can serve many different clients, and any host that speaks the protocol

58
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:01,000
can connect to it. Write your tools and resources once, and they work across every compliant client,

59
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:07,000
not just one application. That's the real payoff over a plain function calling setup,

60
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,000
where your tools are wired into a single app. A standard interface is what lets a whole ecosystem

61
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,000
share the same servers. Keep that in mind as we move into actually building one.

62
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:24,000
An MCP server exposes three primitives, and ownership is the piece to remember. Tools are

63
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:30,000
model-controlled actions. Resources are application-controlled data, and prompts

64
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:36,000
are user-controlled templates. Keep in mind, MCP writes input schema as camel case,

65
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:43,000
while the API writes input schema as snake case with an underscore. That covers the MCP primitives,

66
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:46,000
tools, resources, and prompts. I'll see you in the next one.

